  areaServed={['Formby', 'Freshfield', 'Hightown', 'Ainsdale', 'Crosby', 'Maghull', 'Lydiate']}
>
  <section class="brx-sec brx-tint" id="selling">
    <div class="brx-wrap">
      <h2>Do I need a gas safety certificate to sell in Formby?</h2>
      <p class="brx-answer">Not by law, if you live in the home. But many buyers and solicitors ask for one, and having it ready can keep the sale moving.</p>
      <p>Formby has lots of owner-occupied family homes. So here a gas safety check is often about buying and selling, not only renting.</p>
      <div class="sg-cols">
        <div class="sg-box">
          <h3>Selling</h3>
          <p>Get the check done before you list. If a fault turns up, you can fix it on your terms. The certificate is emailed the same day, ready for your solicitor.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="sg-box">
          <h3>Buying</h3>
          <p>Book a check before you complete, with the seller's permission. A homebuyer survey does not test the gas. If we find a fault, you have it in writing to raise with the seller.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p>Is the boiler over 10 years old? Is there no service history for the hob? Then a £100 check is a small price for peace of mind.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="brx-sec" id="appliances">
    <div class="brx-wrap">
      <h2>How much if I have more than one gas appliance?</h2>
      <p class="brx-answer">The first two appliances are £100 in total. Each extra one is £20. We check them all in one visit, on one certificate.</p>
      <p>Many Formby homes have a boiler plus a gas hob or a range cooker. Some larger homes have more.</p>
      <div class="brx-table-wrap">
        <table class="brx-table">
          <thead><tr><th>What you have</th><th>Price inc VAT</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Boiler only</td><td>£100</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Boiler and gas hob</td><td>£100</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Boiler, gas hob and gas oven</td><td>£120</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Certificate and boiler service</td><td>£199</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <h3>Got a gas fire?</h3>
      <p>Some homes, often older ones in Freshfield, have a gas fire in the lounge. We do not check gas fires. If you do not need it, we can disconnect and cap it safely. If you want to keep it, it must be checked by an engineer registered for gas fires.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="brx-sec brx-tint" id="law">
    <div class="brx-wrap">
      <h2>What must Formby landlords do?</h2>
      <p class="brx-answer">Get a new gas safety check every 12 months, by a Gas Safe registered engineer. It must cover every gas appliance you supply.</p>
      <p>The rules come from the <a href="https://www.hse.gov.uk/gas/landlords/index.htm" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Gas Safety (Installation and Use) Regulations 1998</a>. Sefton Council and the HSE can both act if you do not have one.</p>
      <ul class="sg-ticks">
        <li>Give your tenants a copy within 28 days of the check.</li>
        <li>Give new tenants a copy before they move in.</li>
        <li>Keep each record for at least 2 years.</li>
        <li>Fit a carbon monoxide alarm in any room with a gas boiler or other fixed fuel-burning appliance.</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="brx-note"><p><strong>Larger rental homes:</strong> lets in Formby are often bigger houses with a boiler, a gas hob and sometimes a gas fire. If it was there when the tenant moved in, it is yours to certify.</p></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="brx-sec" id="checks">
    <div class="brx-wrap">
      <h2>What do you check?</h2>
      <p class="brx-answer">We check every gas appliance, the pipework and the air supply. The aim is to make sure nothing can leak gas or carbon monoxide.</p>
      <div class="sg-cols">
        <div class="sg-box">
          <h3>The gas supply</h3>
          <ul class="sg-ticks">
            <li>A tightness test to prove there are no leaks</li>
            <li>Gas pressure at the meter and each appliance</li>
            <li>The emergency control valve works</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="sg-box">
          <h3>Each appliance</h3>
          <ul class="sg-ticks">
            <li>It burns gas safely, with a flue gas test on the boiler</li>
            <li>The flue takes fumes outside</li>
            <li>There is enough fresh air</li>
            <li>Safety devices, like the flame cut-off on a hob, work</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p>Near the coast, we look at the outside flue end for wear. In refitted kitchens, we check the air vents were not lost in the work.</p>
      <h3>What if something fails?</h3>
      <div class="brx-table-wrap">
        <table class="brx-table defl">
          <thead><tr><th>Label</th><th>What happens</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><strong>Immediately dangerous</strong></td><td>A risk to life now. With your consent, we disconnect it until it is fixed.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>At risk</strong></td><td>A fault could become dangerous. With your consent, we turn it off until it is fixed.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Not to current standards</strong></td><td>It met older rules. It can stay in use, and we note it.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>If a repair is needed, we give you the price first. Sellers can fix it before listing. Buyers have it in writing.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="brx-sec brx-tint" id="how">
    <div class="brx-wrap">
      <h2>How does it work?</h2>
      <ol class="sg-steps">
        <li><strong>Call or book online.</strong> Tell us the address, every gas appliance, and if it is for a sale. We give you a fixed price.</li>
        <li><strong>We arrange access.</strong> We agree a time with you, your tenant, your agent or the seller.</li>
        <li><strong>The check.</strong> A Gas Safe registered engineer checks each appliance. Most homes take under an hour.</li>
        <li><strong>Your certificate.</strong> We email it the same day, ready for your solicitor, agent or tenant.</li>
        <li><strong>Next year.</strong> Landlords get a reminder before it runs out.</li>
      </ol>
      <div class="brx-cta">
        <p><strong>Selling soon or need a certificate this week?</strong> Call and we will find the next free slot.</p>
        <a href={TEL} class="brx-btn">Call {PHONE}</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="brx-sec" id="formby">
    <div class="brx-wrap">
      <h2>Where around Formby do you cover?</h2>
      <p class="brx-answer">All of Formby and Freshfield in L37, and the villages along the Sefton coast. The price is the same everywhere.</p>
      <div class="sg-cols">
        <div class="sg-box">
          <h3>Formby and Freshfield</h3>
          <p>Formby village, Freshfield and the rest of L37. Many larger homes with more than one gas appliance.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="sg-box">
          <h3>Nearby</h3>
          <p>Hightown L38, Ainsdale PR8, Crosby L23, and Maghull and Lydiate L31.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="brx-note">
        <p><strong>Landlords also need an EICR.</strong> A gas safety certificate covers the gas. The electrics need an EICR at least every 5 years. Our partner firm <a href="https://www.idealelectricalsolutions.co.uk/eicr-formby">Ideal Electrical Solutions does landlord EICRs in Formby</a> from £149 including VAT, NICEIC approved, with the certificate emailed within 24 hours.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</ServiceGuide>
